{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310677,"nonce":"YSEYH/kYHBRNkYts3O6b/Jf+KB0=","title":"GGUFReader.decodeQ4KM was producing a permuted layout that didn't match Linear's matmul expectations; the matmul kernel sequence (a @ b) ...","message":"**Status: FIXED.** 128/128 haxe regression green; tiny-transformer passes\nwith deterministic generation; the Q4_K_M layout sanity test passes against\na synthetic PyTorch-shaped weight matrix.\n\n\n**Bug 1 — Permuted dequant layout.** GGUFReader.decodeQ4KM set\n`rows = product of all but last dim of GGUF dims`, `cols = dims[last]`.\nGGUF's spec lists dims innermost-first, so for a PyTorch weight `[out, in]`\nthe file's `dims = [in, out]`. Physical memory is PyTorch row-major\n(`out` outer rows × `in` inner cols, Q4_K_M blocks along `in`). The old\ncode interpreted this as `rows=in, cols=out` which is the **transpose** of\nthe physical layout — but the dequant kernel still reads the SAME bytes in\norder, just labelling positions differently. The resulting Tensor's `(r, c)`\nindexing was a permutation of the actual weight matrix. Test fired in\n`runtime/src/quant.rs::dequant_shape_matches_pytorch_weight_layout`:\n130,560 / 131,072 entries wrong under the old convention.\n\nThis never surfaced because nothing exercised the full Q4_K_M → Linear\ninference path: `tiny-transformer` builds synthetic F32 weights, no Rust\ntest exercised `q4_k_m_matmul_f32` end-to-end, and Llama-3 8B never made\nit past tensor load (OOM after the offset fix).\n\n**Bug 2 — Linear's convention couldn't accept a PyTorch-style weight.** The\nexisting `Linear.forward` did `x.matmul(weight)`, expecting weight as\n`[in, out]` (already transposed). With Bug 1 fixed, the dequant'd tensor\nis `[out, in]` (PyTorch shape) — `x.matmul` of that gives the wrong\noutput shape entirely.\n\n\nAdopted PyTorch convention end-to-end: `weight` is `[out, in]` and forward\nis `y = x @ weight.T + bias`. Implemented via a new fused-transpose kernel\nso the matmul still reads row-major both sides (SIMD-friendly).\n\n- `runtime/src/tensor.rs` — added `rayzor_tensor_matmul_t(a, b)` doing\n  `y[i, j] = sum_k a[i, k] * b[j, k]`. F32 fast path is a dot-product per\n  output element; falls back to scalar for non-F32 / strided inputs.\n- `compiler/haxe-std/rayzor/ds/Tensor.hx` — added `tensor.matmulT(other)`.\n- `compiler/src/stdlib/tensor.rs` + `runtime_mapping.rs` — MIR wrapper\n  `Tensor_matmulT` + dispatch.\n- `nue/nue/Linear.hx` — `forward` now does `x.matmulT(weight)`; docstring\n  flipped to document the `[out, in]` PyTorch convention.\n- `nue/nue/loader/GGUFReader.hx` and `nue/nue/loader/GGUFLoader.hx`\n  (`decodeQ4KM`) — swapped to `rows = dims[last] (= out)`,\n  `cols = product of earlier dims (= in)`.\n- `nue/examples/tiny-transformer/Main.hx` — flipped every\n  `deterministicWeight([HIDDEN, X], …)` to `[X, HIDDEN]` to match the new\n  convention.\n\n\nA `.transpose()` view is a stride-only no-copy operation, so semantically\ncorrect, but the F32 matmul fast path bails to the scalar fallback when\neither operand is non-contiguous in its column stride. Inference's hot\npath (a Linear matmul per layer per token) cannot afford the scalar\nfallback. `matmulT` does the dot-product directly on two contiguous row\nbuffers — same FLOP count as the contiguous fast path of regular matmul.\n\n\n- [[bugs_gguf_tensor_decode_crash]] — the i32-offset fix that surfaced\n  this code path. The offset bug had to be fixed first; this layout bug\n  was the *next* obstacle.\n- Phase 4b (`Linear` dispatch through `QTensor.matmulF32` to skip the F32\n  dequant) builds on this fix: the QTensor has the same `[out, in]`\n  layout as the dequant Tensor, so the kernel composes naturally with\n  `Wq.matmulF32(x.T).T`.\n- [[project_nue_tiny_transformer_smoke.md]] — tiny-transformer continues\n  to pass after the convention flip.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_q4_km_layout_and_linear_convention` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}